  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 143 PM MDT Fri Aug 21 2026\n\nThe upper high will then settle slightly southward into Sunday\nwith similar height and wind fields. Deterministic QPF again\nfocuses convective initiation over the western and northern\nmountains Sunday afternoon with minimal storms elsewhere. A\nshortwave trough is modeled on the northern periphery of the high\nin CO, and this could ignite a few storms that sag into northern\nNM, but this feature will likely become more of a player into\nMonday, as it moves into the plains and sends a backdoor front\ninto northeastern NM. \n\nSubtle dewpoint rises and more of an upslope surface wind \ncomponent will lead to better convective initiation on the east \nslopes of the Sangre de Cristos Monday. Subsequent outflow will \nthen potentially spread additional storms farther south and west \nwith minimal changes to the storm coverage elsewhere through\nMonday evening. \n\nBy Tuesday, the GFS continues to keep the northeast favored for \nstorms, although surface winds will have veered and/or lost much \nof their upslope component by then. The long range ensembles also \nlatch on to the northeast and secondarily the western mountains \nfor Tuesday with the high having settled just south of I-40. \n\nThe consensus is for the high to nudge slightly southwestward \ninto Wednesday, bringing more of a northwesterly wind component\naloft to the northeastern corner of NM. This will open the door \nto another moist front that could reintroduce storms to \nnortheastern and east central zones through Wednesday evening. \nMeanwhile, PWATs of 0.6 to 0.8 inch will keep storms going over \nother western and northern high terrain areas, but with spotty \nrainfall footprints. Overall heights are shown to start reducing \nby 1 to 3 decameters Wednesday, but temperatures will still be \nabove average by 5 to 10 degrees in most locales.\n\nFor Thursday, the high is modeled to remain centered over\nsouthwestern NM with only slight reductions in heights (594-596\ndecameters at 500 mb). Storm coverage will depend on recycling of\nprevious day's moisture, but for now PWATs are shown to range \nfrom 0.7 to 1.1 inch with the eastern plains observing higher \nvalues.",
